#: source/glossary.md:1
msgid "Glossary"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:3
msgid "\\[`∅`\\]{#gloss-empty-set}"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:5
msgid ""
"The empty set symbol. In the context of profile history, this denotes a "
"package is not present in a particular version of the profile."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:7
msgid "\\[`ε`\\]{#gloss-epsilon}"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:9
msgid ""
"The epsilon symbol. In the context of a package, this means the version is "
"empty. More precisely, the derivation does not have a version attribute."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:11
msgid "[base directory](#gloss-base-directory){#gloss-base-directory}"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:13
msgid "The location from which relative paths are resolved."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:15
msgid ""
"For expressions in a file, the base directory is the directory containing "
"that file. This is analogous to the directory of a [base URL](https://"
"datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc1808#section-3.3)."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:26
msgid ""
"For expressions written in command line arguments with [`--expr`](./command-"
"ref/opt-common.html#opt-expr), the base directory is the current working "
"directory."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:30
msgid "\\[binary cache\\]{#gloss-binary-cache}"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:32
msgid ""
"A _binary cache_ is a Nix store which uses a different format: its metadata "
"and signatures are kept in `.narinfo` files rather than in a [Nix database]"
"(#gloss-nix-database). This different format simplifies serving store "
"objects over the network, but cannot host builds. Examples of binary caches "
"include S3 buckets and the [NixOS binary cache](https://cache.nixos.org)."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:38
msgid "\\[build system\\]{#gloss-build-system}"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:40
msgid ""
"Generic term for software that facilitates the building of software by "
"automating the invocation of compilers, linkers, and other tools."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:42
msgid ""
"Nix can be used as a generic build system. It has no knowledge of any "
"particular programming language or toolchain. These details are specified in "
"[derivation expressions](#gloss-derivation-expression)."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:46
msgid "[closure](#gloss-closure){#gloss-closure}"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:48
msgid ""
"The closure of a store path is the set of store paths that are directly or "
"indirectly “reachable” from that store path; that is, it’s the closure of "
"the path under the _references_ relation. For a package, the closure of its "
"derivation is equivalent to the build-time dependencies, while the closure "
"of its [output path](#gloss-output-path) is equivalent to its runtime "
"dependencies. For correct deployment it is necessary to deploy whole "
"closures, since otherwise at runtime files could be missing. The command "
"`nix-store --query --requisites ` prints out closures of store paths."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:58
msgid ""
"As an example, if the [store object](#gloss-store-object) at path `P` "
"contains a [reference](#gloss-reference) to a store object at path `Q`, then "
"`Q` is in the closure of `P`. Further, if `Q` references `R` then `R` is "
"also in the closure of `P`."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:62 source/glossary.md:309 source/glossary.md:330
#: source/glossary.md:334 source/glossary.md:345
msgid "See [References](./store/store-object.md#references) for details."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:66
msgid "\\[content address\\]{#gloss-content-address}"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:68
msgid ""
"A [_content address_](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Content-"
"addressable_storage) is a secure way to reference immutable data. The "
"reference is calculated directly from the content of the data being "
"referenced, which means the reference is [_tamper proof_](https://en."
"wikipedia.org/wiki/Tamperproofing) \\--- variations of the data should "
"always calculate to distinct content addresses."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:75
msgid "For how Nix uses content addresses, see:"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:77
msgid ""
"[Content-Addressing File System Objects](./store/file-system-object/content-"
"address.md)"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:78
msgid ""
"[Content-Addressing Store Objects](./store/store-object/content-address.md)"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:79
msgid "[content-addressing derivation](#gloss-content-addressing-derivation)"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:81
msgid ""
"Software Heritage's writing on [_Intrinsic and Extrinsic identifiers_]"
"(https://www.softwareheritage.org/2020/07/09/intrinsic-vs-extrinsic-"
"identifiers) is also a good introduction to the value of content-addressing "
"over other referencing schemes."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:83
msgid ""
"Besides content addressing, the Nix store also uses [input addressing]"
"(#gloss-input-addressed-store-object)."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:85
msgid "\\[content-addressed storage\\]{#gloss-content-addressed-store}"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:87
msgid ""
"The industry term for storage and retrieval systems using [content "
"addressing](#gloss-content-address). A Nix store also has [input addressing]"
"(#gloss-input-addressed-store-object), and metadata."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:89
msgid ""
"\\[content-addressed store object\\]{#gloss-content-addressed-store-object}"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:91
msgid ""
"A [store object](#gloss-store-object) which is [content-addressed](#gloss-"
"content-address), i.e. whose [store path](#gloss-store-path) is determined "
"by its contents. This includes derivations, the outputs of [content-"
"addressing derivations](#gloss-content-addressing-derivation), and the "
"outputs of [fixed-output derivations](#gloss-fixed-output-derivation)."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:95
msgid ""
"See [Content-Addressing Store Objects](./store/store-object/content-address."
"md) for details."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:97
msgid ""
"\\[content-addressing derivation\\]{#gloss-content-addressing-derivation}"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:99
msgid ""
"A derivation which has the [`__contentAddressed`](./language/advanced-"
"attributes.md#adv-attr-__contentAddressed) attribute set to `true`."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:103
msgid "[derivation](#gloss-derivation){#gloss-derivation}"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:105
msgid ""
"A derivation can be thought of as a [pure function](https://en.wikipedia.org/"
"wiki/Pure_function) that produces new [store objects](#gloss-store-object) "
"from existing store objects."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:107
msgid ""
"Derivations are implemented as [operating system processes that run in a "
"sandbox](./store/building.md). This sandbox by default only allows reading "
"from store objects specified as inputs, and only allows writing to "
"designated [outputs](#gloss-output) to be [captured as store objects](./"
"store/building.md#processing-outputs)."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:110
msgid ""
"A derivation is typically specified as a [derivation expression](#gloss-"
"derivation-expression) in the [Nix language](./language/index.md), and "
"[instantiated](#gloss-instantiate) to a [store derivation](#gloss-store-"
"derivation). There are multiple ways of obtaining store objects from store "
"derivations, collectively called [realisation](#gloss-realise)."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:115
msgid ""
"[derivation expression](#gloss-derivation-expression){#gloss-derivation-"
"expression}"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:117
msgid ""
"A description of a [store derivation](#gloss-store-derivation) using the "
"[`derivation` primitive](./language/derivations.md) in the [Nix language](./"
"language/index.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:121
msgid "[derivation path](#gloss-derivation-path){#gloss-derivation-path}"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:123
msgid ""
"A [store path](#gloss-store-path) which uniquely identifies a [store "
"derivation](#gloss-store-derivation)."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:125
msgid ""
"See [Referencing Store Derivations](./store/derivation/index.md#derivation-"
"path) for details."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:127
msgid "Not to be confused with \\[deriving path\\]."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:131
msgid "\\[deriver\\]{#gloss-deriver}"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:133
msgid ""
"The [store derivation](#gloss-store-derivation) that produced an [output "
"path](#gloss-output-path)."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:135
msgid ""
"The deriver for an output path can be queried with the `--deriver` option to "
"[`nix-store --query`](./command-ref/nix-store/query.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:138
msgid "\\[deriving path\\]{#gloss-deriving-path}"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:140
msgid ""
"Deriving paths are a way to refer to [store objects](#gloss-store-object) "
"that might not yet be [realised](#gloss-realise)."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:142
msgid ""
"See [Deriving Path](./store/derivation/index.md#deriving-path) for details."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:144
msgid "Not to be confused with [derivation path](#gloss-derivation-path)."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:146
msgid "\\[directed acyclic graph\\]{#gloss-directed-acyclic-graph}"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:148
msgid ""
"A [directed acyclic graph](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/"
"Directed_acyclic_graph) (DAG) is graph whose edges are given a direction "
"(\"a to b\" is not the same edge as \"b to a\"), and for which no possible "
"path (created by joining together edges) forms a cycle."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:150
msgid ""
"DAGs are very important to Nix. In particular, the non-self-[references]"
"(#gloss-reference) of [store object](#gloss-store-object) form a cycle."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:153
msgid "\\[experimental feature\\]{#gloss-experimental-feature}"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:155
msgid ""
"Not yet stabilized functionality guarded by named experimental feature "
"flags. These flags are enabled or disabled with the [`experimental-features`]"
"(./command-ref/conf-file.html#conf-experimental-features) setting."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:158
msgid ""
"See the contribution guide on the [purpose and lifecycle of experimental "
"feaures](./development/experimental-features.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:160
msgid ""
"[file system object](#gloss-file-system-object){#gloss-file-system-object}"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:162
msgid "The Nix data model for representing simplified file system data."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:164
msgid "See [File System Object](./store/file-system-object.md) for details."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:168
msgid "\\[fixed-output derivation\\]{#gloss-fixed-output-derivation} (FOD)"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:170
msgid ""
"A [store derivation](#gloss-store-derivation) where a cryptographic hash of "
"the [output](#gloss-output) is determined in advance using the [`outputHash`]"
"(./language/advanced-attributes.md#adv-attr-outputHash) attribute, and where "
"the [`builder`](./language/derivations.md#attr-builder) executable has "
"access to the network."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:172
msgid "\\[hermetic\\]{#gloss-hermetic}"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:174
msgid ""
"An evaluation or build process is hermetic when one can mechanically "
"identify the set of all inputs that may affect it. At the build level this "
"is achieved by sandboxing; at the evaluation level by restricting impure "
"access (as in [pure evaluation](./command-ref/conf-file.md#conf-pure-eval)) "
"together with [locking](#gloss-locking) or [pinning](#gloss-pinning) of the "
"fetched inputs, taken transitively over [pure fetches](./command-ref/conf-"
"file.md#pure-fetch)."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:177
msgid "\\[IFD\\]{#gloss-ifd}"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:179
msgid "[Import From Derivation](./language/import-from-derivation.md)"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:181
msgid "\\[impure derivation\\]{#gloss-impure-derivation}"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:183
msgid ""
"[An experimental feature](./development/experimental-features.md#xp-feature-"
"impure-derivations) that allows derivations to be explicitly marked as "
"impure, so that they are always rebuilt, and their outputs not reused by "
"subsequent calls to realise them."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:186
msgid "\\[input-addressed store object\\]{#gloss-input-addressed-store-object}"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:188
msgid ""
"A store object produced by building a non-[content-addressed](#gloss-content-"
"addressing-derivation), non-[fixed-output](#gloss-fixed-output-derivation) "
"derivation."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:193
msgid ""
"See [input-addressing derivation outputs](store/derivation/outputs/input-"
"address.md) for details."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:195
msgid "\\[installable\\]{#gloss-installable}"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:197
msgid "Something that can be realised in the Nix store."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:199
msgid ""
"See [installables](./command-ref/new-cli/nix.md#installables) for [`nix` "
"commands](./command-ref/new-cli/nix.md) (experimental) for details."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:201
msgid "[instantiate](#gloss-instantiate){#gloss-instantiate}, instantiation"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:203
msgid ""
"Translate a [derivation expression](#gloss-derivation-expression) into a "
"[store derivation](#gloss-store-derivation)."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:205
msgid ""
"See [`nix-instantiate`](./command-ref/nix-instantiate.md), which produces a "
"store derivation from a Nix expression that evaluates to a derivation."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:209
msgid "\\[locking\\]{#gloss-locking}"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:211
msgid ""
"In package management, _locking_ is the concept or process of creating a "
"lock file, which maps each mutable evaluation input to an immutable "
"reference, so that future evaluations resolve to the same immutable versions "
"rather than whatever the mutable references currently point to."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:213
msgid "\\[Nix Archive (NAR)\\]{#gloss-nar}"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:215
msgid ""
"A _N_ix _AR_chive. This is a serialisation of a path in the Nix store. It "
"can contain regular files, directories and symbolic links.  NARs are "
"generated and unpacked using `nix-store --dump` and `nix-store --restore`."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:220
msgid ""
"See [Nix Archive](store/file-system-object/content-address.html#serial-nix-"
"archive) for details."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:222
msgid "[Nix database](#gloss-nix-database){#gloss-nix-database}"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:224
msgid ""
"An SQlite database to track [reference](#gloss-reference)s between [store "
"object](#gloss-store-object)s. This is an implementation detail of the "
"[local store](./store/types/local-store.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:227
msgid "Default location: `/nix/var/nix/db`."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:231
msgid "\\[Nix expression\\]{#gloss-nix-expression}"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:233
msgid "A syntactically valid use of the [Nix language](./language/index.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:235 source/glossary.md:242 source/glossary.md:379
msgid "**Example**"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:237
msgid "The contents of a `.nix` file form a Nix expression."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:239
msgid ""
"Nix expressions specify [derivation expressions](#gloss-derivation-"
"expression), which are [instantiated](#gloss-instantiate) into the Nix store "
"as [store derivations](#gloss-store-derivation). These derivations can then "
"be [realised](#gloss-realise) to produce [outputs](#gloss-output)."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:244
msgid ""
"Building and deploying software using Nix entails writing Nix expressions to "
"describe [packages](#package) and compositions thereof."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:246
msgid "\\[Nix instance\\]{#gloss-nix-instance}"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:248
msgid ""
"An installation of Nix, which includes the presence of a [store](#gloss-"
"store), and the Nix package manager which operates on that store. A local "
"Nix installation and a [remote builder](./advanced-topics/distributed-builds."
"md) are two examples of Nix instances."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:250
msgid "A running Nix process, such as the `nix` command."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:252
msgid "[output](#gloss-output){#gloss-output}"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:254
msgid ""
"A [store object](#gloss-store-object) produced by a [store derivation]"
"(#gloss-store-derivation). See [the `outputs` argument to the `derivation` "
"function](./language/derivations.md#attr-outputs) for details."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:259
msgid ""
"\\[output closure\\]{#gloss-output-closure}  \n"
"The [closure](#gloss-closure) of an [output path](#gloss-output-path). It "
"only contains what is \\[reachable\\] from the output."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:262
msgid "[output path](#gloss-output-path){#gloss-output-path}"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:264
msgid ""
"The [store path](#gloss-store-path) to the [output](#gloss-output) of a "
"[store derivation](#gloss-store-derivation)."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:268
msgid "[package](#package){#package}"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:270
msgid ""
"A software package; files that belong together for a particular purpose, and "
"metadata."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:272
msgid ""
"Nix represents files as [file system objects](#gloss-file-system-object), "
"and how they belong together is encoded as [references](#gloss-reference) "
"between [store objects](#gloss-store-object) that contain these file system "
"objects."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:274
msgid ""
"The [Nix language](./language/index.md) allows denoting packages in terms of "
"[attribute sets](./language/types.md#type-attrs) containing:"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:275
msgid ""
"attributes that refer to the files of a package, typically in the form of "
"[derivation outputs](#gloss-output),"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:276
msgid ""
"attributes with metadata, such as information about how the package is "
"supposed to be used."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:278
msgid "The exact shape of these attribute sets is up to convention."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:282
msgid "\\[pinning\\]{#gloss-pinning}"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:284
msgid ""
"Like [locking](#gloss-locking), but a pin only locks a single input. A "
"pinning solution may manage a collection of pins, but serves the bottom-up "
"purpose of fixing an input's reference on demand, whereas locking implies a "
"top down approach where all pins are \"coerced\" into a single place. This "
"\"coercion\" is generally achieved by means of high-level mechanisms such as "
"programming language module systems. Nix does not have such a restrictive "
"module system, as even a flake can use expressions that pin or lock on their "
"own. It does not rely on the lock being total, but on a transitive fetching "
"property; see [hermeticity](#gloss-hermetic)."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:292
msgid "\\[profile\\]{#gloss-profile}"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:294
msgid ""
"A symlink to the current _user environment_ of a user, e.g., `/nix/var/nix/"
"profiles/default`."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:297
msgid "\\[purity\\]{#gloss-purity}"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:299
msgid ""
"The assumption that equal Nix derivations when run always produce the same "
"output. This cannot be guaranteed in general (e.g., a builder can rely on "
"external inputs such as the network or the system time) but the Nix model "
"assumes it."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:304
msgid "\\[reachable\\]{#gloss-reachable}"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:306
msgid ""
"A store path `Q` is reachable from another store path `P` if `Q` is in the "
"_closure_ of the _references_ relation."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:311
msgid "[realise](#gloss-realise){#gloss-realise}, realisation"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:313
msgid "Ensure a [store path](#gloss-store-path) is [valid](#gloss-validity)."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:315
msgid "This can be achieved by:"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:316
msgid ""
"Fetching a pre-built [store object](#gloss-store-object) from a [substituter]"
"(#gloss-substituter)"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:317
msgid ""
"[Building](./store/building.md) the corresponding [store derivation](#gloss-"
"store-derivation)"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:318
msgid ""
"Delegating to a [remote machine](./command-ref/conf-file.md#conf-builders) "
"and retrieving the outputs"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:320
msgid ""
"See [`nix-store --realise`](./command-ref/nix-store/realise.md) for a "
"detailed description of the algorithm."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:322
msgid ""
"See also [`nix-build`](./command-ref/nix-build.md) and [`nix build`](./"
"command-ref/new-cli/nix3-build.md) (experimental)."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:326
msgid "[reference](#gloss-reference){#gloss-reference}"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:328
msgid "An edge from one [store object](#gloss-store-object) to another."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:336
msgid "\\[referrer\\]{#gloss-referrer}"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:338
msgid ""
"A reversed edge from one [store object](#gloss-store-object) to another."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:340
msgid "\\[requisite\\]{#gloss-requisite}"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:342
msgid ""
"A store object \\[reachable\\] by a path (chain of references) from a given "
"[store object](#gloss-store-object). The [closure](#gloss-closure) is the "
"set of requisites."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:347
msgid "[store](#gloss-store){#gloss-store}"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:349
msgid ""
"A collection of [store objects](#gloss-store-object), with operations to "
"manipulate that collection. See [Nix Store](./store/index.md) for details."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:352
msgid ""
"There are many types of stores, see [Store Types](./store/types/index.md) "
"for details."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:356
msgid "[store derivation](#gloss-store-derivation){#gloss-store-derivation}"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:358
msgid ""
"A [derivation](#gloss-derivation) represented as a [store object](#gloss-"
"store-object)."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:360
msgid ""
"See [Store Derivation](./store/derivation/index.md#store-derivation) for "
"details."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:364
msgid "[store object](#gloss-store-object){#gloss-store-object}"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:366
msgid "Part of the contents of a [store](#gloss-store)."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:368
msgid ""
"A store object consists of a [file system object](#gloss-file-system-"
"object), [references](#gloss-reference) to other store objects, and other "
"metadata. It can be referred to by a [store path](#gloss-store-path)."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:371
msgid "See [Store Object](./store/store-object.md) for details."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:375
msgid "[store path](#gloss-store-path){#gloss-store-path}"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:377
msgid ""
"The location of a [store object](#gloss-store-object) in the file system, i."
"e., an immediate child of the Nix store directory."
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:381
msgid "`/nix/store/jf6gn2dzna4nmsfbdxsd7kwhsk6gnnlr-git-2.38.1`"
msgstr ""

#: source/glossary.md:383
